"[2511.16520v1] Saving Foundation Flow-Matching Priors for Inverse Problems (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.16520v1)\nciteturn2485view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.16520v1\",\"lineno\":8}); Total lines: 168\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Machine Learning\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 20 Nov 2025 (this version), latest version 12 May 2026 (cite5†v3 )]\nL9: # Title:Saving Foundation Flow-Matching Priors for Inverse Problems\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Yuxiang Wan , cite7†Ryan Devera , cite8†Wenjie Zhang , cite9†Ju Sun L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Saving Foundation Flow-Matching Priors for Inverse Problems, by Yuxiang Wan and 3 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite10†View PDF cite11†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Foundation flow-matching (FM) models promise a universal prior for solving inverse problems (IPs), yet today they trail behind domain-specific or even untrained priors. How can we unlock their potential? We introduce FMPlug, a plug-in framework that redefines how foundation FMs are used in IPs. FMPlug combines an instance-guided, time-dependent warm-start strategy with a sharp Gaussianity regularization, adding problem-specific guidance while preserving the Gaussian structures.\nL17: This leads to a significant performance boost across image restoration and scientific IPs. Our results point to a path for making foundation FM models practical, reusable priors for IP solving.\nL18: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG); Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV); Image and Video Processing (eess.IV); Signal Processing (eess.SP)\nL19: Cite as:  | cite12†arXiv:2511.16520 [cs.LG]\nL20:    | (or cite13†arXi