te.\nL158: \nL159: Both individuals and organizations that work with arXivLabs have embraced and accepted our values of openness, community, excellence, and user data privacy. arXiv is committed to these values and only works with partners that adhere to them.\nL160: Have an idea for a project that will add value for arXiv's community? cite48†Learn more about arXivLabs†info.arxiv.org .\nL161: \nL162: cite49†Which authors of this paper are endorsers? | Disable MathJax (cite50†What is MathJax?†info.arxiv.org )\nL163: \nL164: We gratefully acknowledge support from our major funders, cite51†member institutions†info.arxiv.org , , and all contributors.\nL165: cite52†About†info.arxiv.org · cite53†Help†info.arxiv.org · cite54†Contact†info.arxiv.org · cite55†Subscribe†info.arxiv.org · cite56†Copyright†info.arxiv.org · cite57†Privacy†info.arxiv.org · cite58†Accessibility†info.arxiv.org · cite59†Operational Status (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org L166: \nL167: Major funding support from\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2511.16426v1] FreqFlow: Long-term forecasting using lightweight flow matching (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.16426v1)\nciteturn2485view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.16426v1\",\"lineno\":8}); Total lines: 166\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Machine Learning\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 20 Nov 2025]\nL9: # Title:FreqFlow: Long-term forecasting using lightweight flow matching\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Seyed Mohamad Moghadas , cite6†Bruno Cornelis , cite7†Adrian Munteanu L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled FreqFlow: Long-term forecasting using lightweight flow matching, by Seyed Mohamad Moghadas and 2 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite8†View PDF cite9†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Multivariate time-series (MTS) forecasting is fundamental to applications ranging from urban mobility and resource management to climate modeling. While recent generative models based on denoising diffusion have advanced state-of-the-art performance in capturing complex data distributions, they suffer from significant computational overhead due to iterative stochastic sampling procedures that limit real-time deployment.\nL17: Moreover, these models can be brittle when handling high-dimensional, non-stationary, and multi-scale periodic patterns characteristic of real-world sensor networks. We introduce FreqFlow, a novel framework that leverages conditional flow matching in the frequency domain for deterministic MTS forecasting.\nL18: Unlike conventional approaches that operate in the time domain, FreqFlow transforms the forecasting problem into the spectral domain, where it learns to model amplitude and phase shifts through a single complex-valued linear layer. This frequency-domain formulation enables the model to efficiently capture temporal dynamics via complex multiplication, corresponding to scaling and temporal translations.\nL19: The resulting architecture is exceptionally lightweight with only 89k parameters - an order of magnitude smaller than competing diffusion-based models-while enabling single-pass deterministic sampling through ordinary differential equation (ODE) integration. Our approach decomposes MTS signals into trend, seasonal, and residual components, with the flow matching mechanism specifically designed for residual learning to enhance long-term forecasting accuracy.\nL20: Extensive experiments on real-world traffic speed, volume, and flow datasets demonstrate that FreqFlow achieves state-of-the-art forecasting performance, on average 7\\% RMSE improvements, while being significantly faster and more parameter-efficient than existing methods\nL21: Comments:  | Accepted at EurIPS, 2025\nL22: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL23: Cite as:  | cite10†arXiv:2511.16426 [cs.LG]\nL24:    | (or cite11†arXiv:2511.16426v1 [cs.LG] for this version)\nL25:    | cite12†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.16426†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL26: ## Submission history\nL27: \nL28: From: Seyed Mohamad Moghadas [cite13†view email ]\nL29: [v1] Thu, 20 Nov 2025 14:50:13 UTC (4,548 KB)\nL30: \nL31: Full-text links:\nL32: \nL33: ## Access Paper:\nL34: \nL35: View a PDF of the paper titled FreqFlow: Long-term forecasting using lightweight flow matching, by Seyed Mohamad Moghadas and 2 other authors\nL36: \nL37:   * cite8†View PDF L38:   * cite9†HTML (experimental) L39:   * cite14†TeX Source L40: \nL41: cite15†view license†creativecommons.org L42: ### Current browse context:\nL43: \nL44: cs.LG\nL45: \nL46: cite16†< prev |   cite17†next > L47: \nL48: cite18†new | cite19†recent | cite20†2025-11 L49: \nL50: Change to browse by:\nL51: \nL52: cite21†cs L53: \nL54: ### References & Citations\nL55: \nL56:   * cite22†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L57:   * cite23†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L58:   * cite24†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L59: \nL60: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL61: \nL62: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL63: \nL64: [Button: ×]\nL65: \nL66: loading...\nL67: \nL68: Data provided by:\nL69: \nL70: ### Bookmark\nL71: \nL72: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL73: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL74: \nL75: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL76: \nL77: Bibliographic Explorer (cite25†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL78: \nL79: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL80: \nL81: Connected Papers (cite26†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com )\nL82: \nL83: [Input] Litmaps Toggle\nL84: \nL85: Litmaps (cite27†What is Litmaps?†www.litmaps.co )\nL86: \nL87: [Input] s