eón Villares , cite11†Anya Sims , cite12†Dylan Cope , cite13†Jarek Liesen , cite14†Lukas Seier , cite15†Theo Wolf , cite16†Uljad Berdica , cite17†Alexander David Goldie , cite18†Aaron Courville , cite19†Karin Sevegnani , cite20†Shimon Whiteson , cite21†Jakob Nicolaus Foerster L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Evolution Strategies at the Hyperscale, by Bidipta Sarkar and 15 other authors\nL14: cite22†View PDF cite23†HTML (experimental) L15: > Abstract:We introduce Evolution Guided General Optimization via Low-rank Learning (EGGROLL), an evolution strategies (ES) algorithm designed to scale backprop-free optimization to large population sizes for modern large neural network architectures with billions of parameters. ES is a set of powerful blackbox optimisation methods that can handle non-differentiable or noisy objectives with excellent scaling potential through parallelisation.\nL16: Na{ï}ve ES becomes prohibitively expensive at scale due to the computational and memory costs associated with generating matrix perturbations $E\\in\\mathbb{R}^{m\\times n}$ and the batched matrix multiplications needed to compute per-member forward passes. EGGROLL overcomes these bottlenecks by generating random matrices $A\\in \\mathbb{R}^{m\\times r},\\ B\\in \\mathbb{R}^{n\\times r}$ with $r\\ll \\min(m,n)$ to form a low-rank matrix perturbation $A B^\\top$ that are used in place of the full-rank perturbation $E$.\nL17: As the overall update is an average across a population of $N$ workers, this still results in a high-rank update but with significant memory and computation savings, reducing the auxiliary storage from $mn$ to $r(m+n)$ per layer and the cost of a forward pass from $\\mathcal{O}(mn)$ to $\\mathcal{O}(r(m+n))$ when compared to full-rank ES. A theoretical analysis reveals our low-rank update converges to the full-rank update at a fast $\\mathcal{O}\\left(\\frac{1}{r}\\right)$ rate.\nL18: Our experiments show that (1) EGGROLL does not compromise the performance of ES in tabula-rasa RL settings, despite being faster, (2) it is competitive with GRPO as a technique for improving LLM reasoning, and (3) EGGROLL enables stable pre-training of nonlinear recurrent language models that operate purely in integer datatypes.\nL19: Comments:  | 48 pages, 12 figures, Website at cite24†this https URL†eshyperscale.github.io L20: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite25†arXiv:2511.16652 [cs.LG]\nL22:    | (or cite26†arXiv:2511.16652v1 [cs.LG] for this version)\nL23:    | cite27†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.16652†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Bidipta Sarkar [cite28†view email ]\nL27: [v1] Thu, 20 Nov 2025 18:56:05 UTC (1,481 KB)\nL28: cite5†[v2] Mon, 16 Feb 2026 18:01:18 UTC (2,048 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled Evolution Strategies at the Hyperscale, by Bidipta Sarkar and 15 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * cite22†View PDF L37:   * cite23†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite29†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite30†view license†creativecommons.org L41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: cs.LG\nL44: \nL45: cite31†< prev |   cite32†next > L46: \nL47: cite33†new | cite34†recent | cite35†2025-11 L48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: cite36†cs L52: cite37†cs.AI L53: \nL54: ### References & Citations\nL55: \nL56:   * cite38†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L57:   * cite39†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L58:   * cite40†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L59: \nL60: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL61: \nL62: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL63: \nL64: [Button: ×]\nL65: \nL66: loading...\nL67: \nL68: Data provided by:\nL69: \nL70: ### Bookmark\nL71: \nL72: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL73: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL74: \nL75: [Inp